{"name":"Acacia Haze","hex":"#969c92","fetched":"2026-10-19","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"acacia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub or tree of the tribe Acacieae. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","The thickened or dried juice of several species in Acacieae, in particular Vachellia nilotica (syn. Acacia nilotica, Egyptian acacia)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A roll or bag, filled with dust, borne by Byzantine emperors, as a memento of mortality. It is represented on medals."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/acacia"},{"term":"haze","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Very fine solid particles (smoke, dust) or liquid droplets (moisture) suspended in the air, slightly limiting visibility.","A reduction of transparency of a clear gas or liquid."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To be or become hazy, or thick with haze."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/haze"}],"entries":[{"title":"Acacia","blocks":[{"text":"Acacia, commonly known as wattles or acacias, is a genus of about 1,084 species of shrubs and trees in the subfamily Mimosoideae of the pea family Fabaceae. Initially, it comprised a group of plant species native to Africa, South America, and Australasia, but is now reserved for species mainly from Australia, with others from New Guinea, Southeast Asia, and the Indian Ocean."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG/330px-Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acacia","wikidata":"Q81666"},{"title":"Haze","blocks":[{"text":"Haze is traditionally an atmospheric phenomenon in which dust, smoke, and other dry particulates suspended in air obscure visibility and the clarity of the sky. The World Meteorological Organization manual of codes includes a classification of particulates causing horizontal obscuration into categories of fog, ice fog, steam fog, mist, haze, smoke, volcanic ash, dust, sand, and snow. Sources for particles that cause haze include farming, traffic, industry, windy weather, volcanic activity and wildfires.\nSeen from afar and depending on the direction of view with respect to the Sun, haze may appear brownish or bluish, while mist tends to be bluish grey instead. Whereas haze often is considered a phenomenon occurring in dry air, mist formation is a phenomenon in saturated, humid air. However, haze particles may act as condensation nuclei that leads to the subsequent vapor condensation and formation of mist droplets; such forms of haze are known as \"wet haze\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/MosqueinAbuja.jpg/330px-MosqueinAbuja.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haze","wikidata":"Q643546"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG/500px-Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG/1280px-Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG/500px-Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAcacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg/500px-Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg/1280px-Acacia_dealbata_kz09.